From 2d969dff1d677919b9641dc6dffdb36215768283 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: zswll2 <zswll2@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 23:43:48 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 28/32] fix(desktop): touch long-press reorders sessions
 without stealing scrolls
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The sessions list shared one PointerSensor (6px activation) across mouse and
touch, and the reorder listeners sit on the whole row: on a phone a 6px drag
arm wrestled the vertical scroll away. Touch now gets its own lane —
sidebarReorderSensors() picks TouchSensor with a 400ms/5px long-press
constraint on coarse pointers (swipes stay native scrolls; pan-y on the
rows keeps the browser owning vertical pans) and keeps the unchanged
PointerSensor + KeyboardSensor pair on fine pointers. Reorderable rows also
suppress the iOS long-press callout loupe, and a touch-activated drag
confirms itself with a tap haptic through the existing gesture gate.

The pan-y/callout rule lives unlayered in styles.css: Tailwind utilities
are @layer'd and lose to the unlayered 'button { touch-action: manipulation }'
rule they must override.
